---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SocialButtons from '../components/SocialButtons.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';

const courses = [
  { name: 'Fundamental Algorithms', why: 'rigorous algorithmic problem solving' },
  { name: 'Programming Languages', why: 'programming paradigms and how languages work' },
  { name: 'Machine Learning', why: 'ML with more rigor than I had before' },
  { name: 'Building LLM Reasoners', why: 'building and training an LLM from the ground up on GPUs' },
  { name: 'GPU Programming and Architecture', why: 'how computation happens inside a GPU' },
  { name: 'Realtime Big Data Analytics, Big Data Application Development', why: 'distributed systems, Kafka, and Spark' },
  { name: 'Technologies for Finance', why: 'financial systems and high-performance systems' },
];

const loop = [
  'Understand the problem',
  'Understand the existing system',
  'Reason about the design',
  'Discuss the tradeoffs',
  'Make the engineering decision',
  'Implement it',
  'Measure the impact',
  'Improve',
];

const principles = [
  'understand why it works',
  'systems that hold up in production',
  'measure before optimizing',
  'AI inside real software',
];

const ln = (n: number) => `<span class="ln">${String(n).padStart(2, '0')}</span>`;
const codeHtml = [
  `${ln(1)}<span class="kw">const</span> principles = [`,
  ...principles.map((p, i) => `${ln(i + 2)}  <span class="str">"${p}"</span>,`),
  `${ln(principles.length + 2)}];`,
].join('\n');

const storyNav = [
  { id: 'start', title: 'Where it started' },
  { id: 'college', title: 'College' },
  { id: 'ezee', title: 'Ezee.ai' },
  { id: 'nyu', title: 'NYU' },
  { id: 'how', title: 'How I like to work' },
  { id: 'next', title: "What's next" },
];
---
<Base title="About" description="About Siva Balan: how a software engineer got into computer science through problem solving, from competitive programming to production systems at Ezee.ai and an MS at NYU Courant.">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>About</h1>
    </header>

    <section class="section" aria-label="Introduction">
      <div class="section-label">
        <p class="note">From India, now in New York.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="section-body about-text">
        <p>
          I started programming in 8th grade because I enjoyed solving problems. What kept me
          interested wasn't just getting the answer, but understanding why a particular algorithm
          worked and how it solved the problem efficiently. That curiosity took me from competitive
          programming and CS fundamentals to building production systems at Ezee.ai, where
          algorithms, databases, distributed systems, and performance became real engineering
          problems.
        </p>
        <p>
          At NYU, I've been going deeper into algorithms, distributed systems, machine learning,
          LLMs, and GPU programming. Now I'm looking for work where I can keep solving difficult
          problems, learn from experienced engineers, and build systems that are useful to real
          people.
        </p>

        <figure class="principles" aria-label="Engineering principles: understand why it works; systems that hold up in production; measure before optimizing; AI inside real software.">
          <pre aria-hidden="true"><code set:html={codeHtml} /></pre>
        </figure>

        <SocialButtons />

        <nav class="chapters" aria-label="My story">
          <span class="chapters-label note">My story</span>
          {storyNav.map((c) => <a class="u" href={`#${c.id}`}>{c.title}</a>)}
        </nav>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section chapter" id="start" aria-labelledby="start-h">
      <div class="section-label">
        <h2 id="start-h">Where it started</h2>
        <p class="note">8th grade</p>
      </div>
      <div class="section-body story">
        <p>
          I started learning programming around 8th grade, at my parents' computer teaching
          institution. I began with C, then moved to C++, Java, and Python, and later explored web
          development and machine learning. Most of it happened outside school, in the evenings and
          during summer breaks.
        </p>
        <p>
          I chose computer science because of problem solving. I liked working out how to solve a
          problem, and then how to solve it efficiently in time and space.
        </p>
        <p>
          At first I struggled a lot, especially with competitive programming. I would keep trying
          and still not find the solution. Then I realized that different problems call for
          different techniques, data structures, and algorithms, and that these could be learned.
        </p>
        <p>But getting the correct answer was never the part I cared about most. I wanted to know:</p>
        <ul class="questions">
          <li>Why does this algorithm work?</li>
          <li>Why was the problem approached this way?</li>
          <li>What is the underlying idea?</li>
          <li>How can I learn to think like that?</li>
        </ul>
        <p>That way of thinking is still one of the things I enjoy most about computer science.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section chapter" id="college" aria-labelledby="college-h">
      <div class="section-label">
        <h2 id="college-h">College</h2>
        <p class="note">Amrita Vishwa Vidyapeetham, 2018 – 2022</p>
      </div>
      <div class="section-body story">
        <p>
          In college I studied databases, operating systems, data structures and algorithms,
          computer networks, cloud computing, and deep learning.
        </p>
        <p>
          What I enjoyed was understanding the logic behind how these systems work. Database
          normalization is a good example. It wasn't just a concept to memorize: I could see why
          databases are designed that way, and how that design affects structure, correctness, and
          performance.
        </p>
        <p>Somewhere in those years I realized I had picked the right field.</p>
        <p class="aside-link"><a class="u always" href="/education">Education, with full coursework <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section chapter" id="ezee" aria-labelledby="ezee-h">
      <div class="section-label">
        <h2 id="ezee-h">Ezee.ai</h2>
        <p class="note">Veefin / Ezee.ai, 2022 – 2025</p>
      </div>
      <div class="section-body story">
        <p>At Ezee.ai, the things I had learned started connecting to real systems and real users.</p>
        <p>
          As I gained experience, I started designing features, working across web applications and
          microservices, and making engineering decisions. Problem solving got much broader, and the
          questions changed:
        </p>
        <ul class="questions">
          <li>How should this feature be designed?</li>
          <li>What architecture makes sense here?</li>
          <li>How will it perform?</li>
          <li>How does MongoDB work internally, and why does this query behave the way it does?</li>
          <li>How can I optimize it?</li>
          <li>What are the tradeoffs between these designs?</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          System design started to feel like problem solving from a different angle. Instead of one
          algorithmic problem, I had a real problem to understand. I would study the existing system,
          discuss the design with other engineers, make a decision, implement it, and see the effect
          on real users.
        </p>
        <p class="detail">
          One example: a batch pipeline that took 180 minutes. Working out how MongoDB was actually
          executing it, then reworking the aggregations and indexes, adding caching, and switching to
          bulk writes, brought it down to 20.
        </p>
        <p>
          Ezee.ai also taught me things that weren't technical. I learned from my mistakes, from
          working with teammates, and from discussing problems with senior engineers and executives.
          I learned to take responsibility, and I saw how engineering decisions affect customers.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section chapter" id="nyu" aria-labelledby="nyu-h">
      <div class="section-label">
        <h2 id="nyu-h">NYU</h2>
        <p class="note">MS Computer Science, Courant, 2025 – 2027</p>
      </div>
      <div class="section-body story">
        <p>
          After a few years of practical experience, I wanted to understand the foundations behind
          the systems I was working with. So I came to NYU Courant for a master's, looking for
          rigorous coursework. These are the courses that shaped it:
        </p>
        <dl class="rows courses">
          {courses.map((c) => (
            <div><dt>{c.name}</dt><dd>{c.why}</dd></div>
          ))}
        </dl>
        <p>
          Most of these are areas I had already met at work from a different angle. Here I got to go
          underneath them.
        </p>
        <p>
          I've also been reading the papers behind distributed data systems: MapReduce, Bigtable,
          ZooKeeper, Kafka, Spark, Flink, and others. My notes on them are in
          <a class="text-link" href="/notes">Systems notes</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section chapter" id="how" aria-labelledby="how-h">
      <div class="section-label">
        <h2 id="how-h">How I like to work</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="section-body story">
        <p>The way I like to approach engineering problems is fairly simple:</p>
        <ol class="loop">
          {loop.map((step) => <li>{step}</li>)}
        </ol>
        <p class="loop-note note">and then start again.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section chapter" id="next" aria-labelledby="next-h">
      <div class="section-label">
        <h2 id="next-h">What's next</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="section-body story">
        <p>
          Small problems or problems at much larger scale, I want to keep solving them for real
          users. I want to keep learning, build stronger engineering judgment, and become an engineer
          who contributes not only by writing code, but by understanding systems deeply and making
          good design decisions.
        </p>
        <p>
          I'm looking for full-time software engineering roles in the US where I can keep growing in
          this direction. If that sounds like your team, <a class="text-link" href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>email me</a>.
        </p>
        <SocialButtons />
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .about-text p {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 1.12rem + 0.45vw, 1.5rem);
    line-height: 1.5;
    max-width: 36rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.2em;
  }
  .principles { margin: 2.25rem 0 2rem; max-width: 26rem; }
  .principles pre {
    margin: 0; padding: 1rem 1.25rem 1.05rem;
    background: var(--panel); border-radius: 6px;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
    font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.8125rem; line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--ink); overflow-x: auto;
  }
  .principles :global(.ln) { display: inline-block; width: 2.25rem; color: var(--faint); user-select: none; }
  .principles :global(.kw) { color: var(--muted); }
  .principles :global(.str) { color: var(--accent); }
  .about-text .chapters { margin-top: 2.5rem; }
  .chapters-label { width: 100%; margin-bottom: 0.25rem; }
  .aside-link { font-size: 0.9375rem !important; margin-top: 1.5rem; }
  .story .social { margin-top: 1.5rem; }
  .chapters {
    display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.5rem 1.75rem;
    margin-top: 2.5rem; padding-top: 1.25rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }
  .chapters a { color: var(--muted); }
  .chapters a:hover { color: var(--ink); }

  .story { max-width: 38rem; }
  .story p { font-size: 1.125rem; line-height: 1.7; }

  .questions {
    list-style: none; margin: 0.25rem 0 1.6em; padding: 0.25rem 0 0.25rem 1.25rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
  }
  .questions li {
    font-family: var(--serif); font-style: italic;
    font-size: clamp(1.1875rem, 1.1rem + 0.3vw, 1.3125rem); line-height: 1.4;
    margin: 0 0 0.55rem;
  }
  .questions li:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  .story .detail {
    font-size: 1rem; color: var(--muted);
    background: var(--panel); border-radius: 6px; padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
  }

  .courses { margin: 0.5rem 0 1.6em; }
  .courses > div { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 15rem) minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .courses dt { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 500; }
  .courses dd { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.9375rem; line-height: 1.75; }

  .loop { list-style: none; margin: 0.75rem 0 0; padding: 0; counter-reset: step; position: relative; }
  .loop::before {
    content: ''; position: absolute; left: 0.875rem; top: 0.9rem; bottom: 0.9rem;
    width: 1px; background: var(--rule);
  }
  .loop li {
    counter-increment: step; position: relative;
    display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 1rem;
    padding: 0.4rem 0;
    font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 1.25rem; line-height: 1.35;
  }
  .loop li::before {
    content: counter(step);
    flex: none; width: 1.75rem; height: 1.75rem;
    display: inline-grid; place-items: center;
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.75rem; color: var(--muted);
    background: var(--bg); border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 50%;
    position: relative; top: -0.15rem;
  }
  .loop li:last-child::before { border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--accent); }
  .loop-note { margin: 0.5rem 0 1.75rem 2.75rem; }

  @media (max-width: 46rem) {
    .story p { font-size: 1.0625rem; }
    .courses > div { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .loop li { font-size: 1.125rem; }
  }
</style>

